  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported travel behaviors, which may introduce reporting bias.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the relationship between the built environment and active commuting among 1,419 university students across six campuses in Chile and Spain. Using QGIS, the researchers evaluated eight macro-scale built environment features and compared them against self-reported travel behaviors. Students at campuses with highly activity-friendly environments had 4.76 times higher odds of active commuting in Chile, and over 3.55 times higher odds at two campuses in Spain, compared to their counterparts at less activity-friendly campuses.",
      "why": "It establishes a direct, quantified link between campus-level urban design and active student transit across different national contexts, demonstrating that macro-scale campus environments are a significant determinant of sustainable commuting habits."
    }
